// A generic type's parameter is carried by the **instance**, so a self-less member of that type has
// nothing to read it from. That is a compile error, and deliberately a *different* one from the
// blanket "generics are erased": the instantiation does exist at run time here — it is on the
// receiver's reflected type tag — this member simply has no receiver. The old wording sent the
// author looking for a fix (pass the name in as a parameter) that is not the one (read `self`).
//
// Reported rather than answered, because the alternative is a silent wrong name flowing into
// whatever keyed on it. The E0047 below is the ordinary consequence of a self-less body: an
// inherent method that never reads `self` is an associated function.
// expect: error E0058 at 24:28
// expect: error E0047 at 28:6
// expect: exit 1
struct Todo {
    id: int
}
class Repo<T> {
    pub tbl: string

    fn new(tbl: string): Repo<T> {
        return Repo { tbl: tbl };
    }

    fn model(): string {
        return type_name::<T>();
    }
}
rt: Repo<Todo> = Repo.new("todos");
echo rt.model();
